<BaseLayout title="Environmental Justice & Industrial Hog Farming" description="A collaborative North Carolina Summer Policy Institute analysis of industrial hog farming, environmental exposure, land use, and policy options.">
<article class="hog"><header><div class="container"><a class="back" href="/research-writing/">← Research &amp; Writing</a><div class="eyebrow">Environmental policy analysis / Historical project</div><h1>Environmental Justice<br/><em>in Eastern North Carolina.</em></h1><div class="institute"><span>Policy analysis · 2018</span><a href="https://ncbudget.org/ncspi/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">North Carolina Summer Policy Institute · NC Budget &amp; Tax Center ↗</a></div></div></header>
<section class="intro"><div class="container split"><div><span class="marker">01 / The question</span><h2>Who bears the environmental costs of production?</h2></div><div><p>Industrial livestock production supports a major agricultural economy, but concentrated operations also create challenges involving waste management, air and water quality, and the communities living nearby.</p><p>At the North Carolina Summer Policy Institute, our team researched environmental justice concerns surrounding industrial hog farms in eastern North Carolina. We examined waste management, nearby communities, and the policy choices involved in regulating these facilities.</p></div></div></section>
<figure class="hog-photo"><div class="container"><img src="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/NRCSNC00011_-_North_Carolina_(5131)(NRCS_Photo_Gallery).jpg?width=1400" loading="lazy" alt="USDA Natural Resources Conservation Service photo of an animal waste lagoon in North Carolina" /><figcaption>An animal-waste lagoon photographed in North Carolina. Illustrates the type of infrastructure discussed; not identified as one of our research sites. Bob Nichols / USDA NRCS. <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:NRCSNC00011_-_North_Carolina_(5131)(NRCS_Photo_Gallery).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Source ↗</a></figcaption></div></figure><section class="exposure"><div class="container"><span class="marker">02 / Exposure pathways</span><h2>How waste moves beyond the farm.</h2><div class="path"><div><strong>Concentrated operations</strong><p>Large numbers of animals produce substantial amounts of waste that must be stored, treated, and managed.</p></div><span aria-hidden="true">→</span><div><strong>Storage &amp; land application</strong><p>The project examined waste lagoons and application to agricultural land as potential sources of emissions and runoff.</p></div><span aria-hidden="true">→</span><div><strong>Environmental concerns</strong><p>Nearby residents may experience odors, air-quality impacts, and risks related to water contamination.</p></div></div><p class="source">Conceptual pathways summarized from the original team presentation; this is not a measurement of exposure at any specific property.</p></div></section>
<section class="questions"><div class="container split"><div><span class="marker">03 / Environmental justice</span><h2>Location matters.</h2></div><div><p>Our analysis considered concerns about the proximity of industrial hog operations to lower-income communities and communities of color. Beyond the environmental mechanisms, we examined how industrial land-use decisions can distribute risks unevenly.</p><p>The policy question was not only how waste is handled, but also how affected communities are protected and how decisions are made.</p></div></div></section>
<section class="options"><div class="container"><span class="marker">04 / Policy options considered</span><h2>Policy options we considered.</h2><div class="option-list"><div><span>01</span><h3>Waste infrastructure</h3><p>Explore treatment improvements and possible waste-to-resource technologies, weighing environmental benefits against costs and implementation capacity.</p></div><div><span>02</span><h3>Land use &amp; setbacks</h3><p>Consider minimum distances between waste operations and residences, including the practical constraints for existing farms.</p></div><div><span>03</span><h3>Production &amp; oversight</h3><p>Examine limits on facility concentration and stronger standards, recognizing enforcement demands and the economic role of agriculture.</p></div><div><span>04</span><h3>Community protection</h3><p>Consider compensation, health-related assistance, and remediation alongside measures intended to prevent future harm.</p></div></div></div></section>
<section class="close"><div class="container split"><div><span class="marker">05 / Reflection</span><h2>Policy lives in the details of implementation.</h2></div><div><p>Our presentation weighed environmental and community concerns against regulatory processes, existing legislation, and the industry's economic importance. The exercise connected environmental research to the practical constraints of public decision-making.</p><p>It remains part of my interest in land use, resource allocation, environmental data, and the ways policy decisions affect communities.</p><p class="archive">This is a historical collaborative policy exercise, not a current regulatory review. Our team developed potential interventions but did not implement or evaluate them.</p></div></div></section>
